    options: ['MVI is only for server-side apps', 'It requires Android Intents', 'MVI uses a single immutable state object, user actions as intents, and a reducer that produces new state from current state + intent', 'It is the same as MVVM'],
    correctAnswer: 2,
    explanation: 'MVI provides a strict unidirectional data flow. The View renders a single State object. User actions dispatch Intents (events). A reducer function takes current State + Intent and produces a new State. This makes state changes predictable and testable.',
    codeSnippet: '// State\ndata class CounterState(val count: Int = 0, val isLoading: Boolean = false)\n\n// Intent (user actions)\nsealed class CounterIntent {\n    object Increment : CounterIntent()\n    object Decrement : CounterIntent()\n    object Reset : CounterIntent()\n}\n\nclass CounterViewModel : ViewModel() {\n    private val _state = MutableStateFlow(CounterState())\n    val state = _state.asStateFlow()\n\n    fun processIntent(intent: CounterIntent) {\n        _state.update { currentState ->\n            when (intent) {\n                CounterIntent.Increment -> currentState.copy(count = currentState.count + 1)\n                CounterIntent.Decrement -> currentState.copy(count = currentState.count - 1)\n                CounterIntent.Reset -> CounterState()\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}',
    tags: ['mvi', 'architecture', 'state', 'unidirectional'],
