    explanation: 'The retryWhen operator catches upstream exceptions and decides whether to retry. You can implement exponential backoff by increasing the delay with each attempt. The retry operator is simpler for basic retry logic.',
    codeSnippet: 'fun <T> Flow<T>.retryWithExponentialBackoff(\n    maxRetries: Int = 3,\n    initialDelay: Long = 1000,\n    maxDelay: Long = 16000,\n    factor: Double = 2.0\n): Flow<T> = retryWhen { cause, attempt ->\n    if (attempt < maxRetries && cause is IOException) {\n        val delayMs = (initialDelay * factor.pow(attempt.toInt())).toLong()\n            .coerceAtMost(maxDelay)\n        delay(delayMs)\n        true // retry\n    } else {\n        false // give up\n    }\n}\n\n// Usage\nrepository.fetchData()\n    .retryWithExponentialBackoff()\n    .catch { emit(emptyList()) }\n    .collect { data -> updateUI(data) }',
